avenue in the heart of tunisia's  diplomatic area  has long been the stage for protests and  political change  and this week was no different  security forces lined the streets and  the army has set up barricades  blocking people and politicians from  accessing government buildings  president kaizeid sacked the prime  minister  other prominent cabinet members and  announced a freeze on parliament for 30  days  he says the decision was taken after  following the process laid out  by the constitution but the move has  divided opinions  if the people see that kaiser wants to  become a dictator and work against the  revolution  of course the people rise up against him  and overthrow him  it is really a rescue operation because  lately all tunisians have seen that the  economic and social situation has  deteriorated a lot  under the government's policies the  biggest political group the anahata  party  is calling the president's move a coup  but says it's  open and ready for dialogue  we reject these unconstitutional  announcements and we  welcome all the rejections that have  come from  most other tunisian political parties as  well as civil society organizations  and we call on the tunisian people to  be calm and vigilant and be ready to  defend  their democracy  mass protests were held on sunday with  many focusing their anger at the anata  party  a long struggle with covert 19 and an  economy in decline  has become a perfect recipe for unrest  then on monday a face-off in tunis  between those for  and against the president these same  streets were filled during the 2011  jasmine revolution that led to a decade  of democracy  but the people now in power are being  accused of corruption  and incompetence  [Applause]  and the president is accused of  establishing an authoritarian government  that many people here fought so hard to  rid of a decade ago  for now what is important is that the  decision for all the  political actions to sit together that  we are no longer within an authoritarian  regime  in tunisia but we are in democratic  regime in tunisia and everything has to  happen within that democratic process  and democratic  framework for now tunis is calm many  don't know whether  its fragile democracy can be saved by  dialogue  or whether the streets will once again  become a stage  for a nation demanding change laura  burton manly al jazeera
 

